Subject: [PATCH 51/62] verimark: IT 0x96 03 commit builds the full 443-byte
 record (Windows-decoded)
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

The IT enroll commit body is a 443-byte structured record (TUID +
WINBIO SID identity section + opaque 326-byte DPAPI blob), NOT a
bare TUID. Decoded byte-for-byte from the 2026-06-07 cdb capture in
WUDFHost — see docs/it-enroll-commit-decode.md.

proto.h/proto.c :
- new builder verimark_it_build_enroll_commit_record() that lays
  out the record per the spec : version + TUID + identity tag +
  WINBIO SID (type 3, 28-byte SID + 40-byte pad) + data tag +
  counter + flag + blob
- new builder verimark_it_build_enroll_commit_payload() that wraps
  the record with the 12-byte wire framing (sub-cmd u32 LE +
  field0 + length u32 LE)
- new constant VERIMARK_IT_ENROLL_SYNTHETIC_SID — synthetic
  S-1-5-21-1-1-1-1000 used by default (the IT firmware does NOT
  validate SID semantics, only the structure)
- 27/27 TDD tests in tests/test_it_enroll_commit.c — every fixed
  offset and length is asserted, plus boundary + error paths

verimark.c : ENR_S_COMMIT_CALL IT branch now calls the builder
and sends the full 455-byte payload (12 framing + 443 record).
The Python reference implementation
tools/verimark-it/enroll_commit.py + tests/test_it_enroll_commit.py
(27/27 green) cross-validate the format byte-for-byte.

Hypothesis under test runtime : the IT firmware stores the DPAPI
blob opaquely (it has no DPAPI key) and the match-on-chip uses
the prior 0x96 02 add_image accumulator. If true, sending zeros
for the 326-byte blob is sufficient for the template to persist
and 0x99 to match. If the firmware validates the blob, we'll see
a non-0x0000 status and have to enrich the body further.
